.page hosts 5 names that are known without asking a name server

# DESCRIPTION
`/etc/hosts` maps names to IPv4 and IPv6 addresses before the DNS is consulted. Each line holds an address followed by one or more names, separated by blanks or tabs; `#` begins a comment. The first name on a line is the official one and is what a reverse lookup (`getnameinfo`, `gethostbyaddr`) returns.

    127.0.0.1   localhost localhost.localdomain
    127.0.0.1   gensokyo
    ::1         localhost ip6-localhost ip6-loopback
    ::1         gensokyo
    10.0.2.2    yukari-gate

Names are compared without regard to case. A name that has lines of both families (`localhost` above) gives both addresses to `getaddrinfo`, which puts them in the order of RFC 6724 (`::1` first, as on other systems); `gethostbyname` and a program that asks for `AF_INET` only get the IPv4 one. The file is read every time a name is looked up, so a change takes effect at once.

# FILES
@ /etc/hosts
    This file (the system ships with `localhost` and `gensokyo`, for both families).

# SEE ALSO
resolv.conf(5), nslookup(1), network(7)
